You can turn off the concurrency check for individual entities by setting the entity's state to <code>EntityState.Modified</code> before calling <code>SaveChanges()</code>. This can be done using the <code>Entry()</code> method of the <code>DbContext</code> and setting the <code>State</code> property. Here's an example:

<pre><code>var existingEntity = new Foo { Id = 1 };
Context.Entry(existingEntity).State = EntityState.Modified; // turn off concurrency check
Context.Set&lt;Foo&gt;().Remove(existingEntity);
Context.SaveChanges();
</code></pre>

In this example, we're setting the state of the entity to <code>EntityState.Modified</code> to tell EF to not check for concurrency when deleting the entity. This should allow you to delete entities that don't exist without throwing a <code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code>. Note that this approach will turn off the concurrency check for all properties of the entity, so use it with caution.

Alternatively, you can catch the <code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code> and handle it in your code. One way to do this is to check the exception message and determine whether it's a concurrency issue or not. If it's not a concurrency issue (e.g. because the entity doesn't exist), you can ignore the exception and continue with the next operation. Here's an example:

<pre><code>try
{
    Context.Set&lt;Foo&gt;().Remove(new Foo { Id = 1 });
    Context.SaveChanges();
}
catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex)
{
    if (!ex.Message.Contains("database operation expected to affect 1 row(s)"))
    {
        throw; // re-throw the exception if it's not a concurrency issue
    }
}
</code></pre>

In this example, we're catching the <code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code> and checking whether the message contains the string "database operation expected to affect 1 row(s)". If it doesn't, we re-throw the exception (because it's not a concurrency issue). If it does, we assume that the entity doesn't exist and ignore the exception.

