Here's a simple Swing code that adds a JButton in a column of a JTable using TableCellRenderer and TableCellEditor:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import javax.swing.table.*;

public class JTableButtonDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("JTable Button Demo");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        JTable table = new JTable(new ButtonTableModel());
        TableColumn column = table.getColumnModel().getColumn(2);
        column.setCellRenderer(new ButtonCellRenderer());
        column.setCellEditor(new ButtonCellEditor(new JCheckBox()));

        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(table);
        frame.getContentPane().add(scrollPane);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }

    private static class ButtonTableModel extends AbstractTableModel {
        private Object[][] data = {
            {"Row 1, Column 1", "Row 1, Column 2", "Button 1"},
            {"Row 2, Column 1", "Row 2, Column 2", "Button 2"},
            {"Row 3, Column 1", "Row 3, Column 2", "Button 3"},
            {"Row 4, Column 1", "Row 4, Column 2", "Button 4"}
        };

        private String[] columnNames = {"Column 1", "Column 2", "Button Column"};

        public int getColumnCount() {
            return columnNames.length;
        }

        public int getRowCount() {
            return data.length;
        }

        public Object getValueAt(int row, int col) {
            return data[row][col];
        }

        public String getColumnName(int col) {
            return columnNames[col];
        }

        public Class getColumnClass(int col) {
            return getValueAt(0, col).getClass();
        }

        public boolean isCellEditable(int row, int col) {
            return true;
        }

        public void setValueAt(Object value, int row, int col) {
            data[row][col] = value;
            fireTableCellUpdated(row, col);
        }
    }

    private static class ButtonCellRenderer extends DefaultTableCellRenderer {

        public ButtonCellRenderer() {
            setHorizontalAlignment(JLabel.CENTER);
        }

        public Component getTableCellRendererComponent(JTable table, Object value,
                boolean isSelected, boolean hasFocus, int row, int column) {

            if (value instanceof JButton) {
                return (JButton) value;
            }

            return super.getTableCellRendererComponent(table, value, isSelected,
                    hasFocus, row, column);
        }
    }

    private static class ButtonCellEditor extends DefaultCellEditor {

        private JButton button;

        public ButtonCellEditor(JCheckBox checkBox) {
            super(checkBox);
            button = new JButton();
            button.setOpaque(true);
            button.addActionListener(e -> fireEditingStopped());
        }

        public Component getTableCellEditorComponent(JTable table, Object value,
                boolean isSelected, int row, int col) {

            if (value instanceof String) {
                button.setText((String) value);
            }
            return button;
        }

        public Object getCellEditorValue() {
            return button;
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we first create a JTable with a custom TableModel (ButtonTableModel). The ButtonTableModel contains three columns: Column 1, Column 2, and Button Column. The Button Column contains the text for the button.

We then use the getColumnModel() method to get the TableColumn object for the Button Column. We set a custom cell renderer and cell editor for this column using setCellRenderer() and setCellEditor() methods. The ButtonCellRenderer class is used as the cell renderer, while the ButtonCellEditor class is used as the cell editor.

The ButtonCellRenderer class is a subclass of DefaultTableCellRenderer and overrides the getTableCellRendererComponent() method. If the value of the cell is a JButton, it just returns the button itself; otherwise, it just calls the superclass implementation.

The ButtonCellEditor class is a subclass of DefaultCellEditor and overrides the getTableCellEditorComponent() and getCellEditorValue() methods. In getTableCellEditorComponent(), it sets the text of the button to the value of the cell, which is assumed to be a String. It then returns the button. In getCellEditorValue(), it just returns the button itself.

When the user clicks on the button, an event is fired, and the editing is stopped by calling fireEditingStopped() method. The returned value of getCellEditorValue() (i.e., the button) is saved back to the cell.

