In Java, a callback is a reference to a method that is passed as an argument to another method. The method receiving the callback expects to execute this callback at some point within its own logic. The callback method allows the caller to specify a behavior that will be executed later.

Here's a simple example of a callback method in Java:

```java
public class Example {

    public void doSomethingWithCallback(Callback callback) {
        // do some work
        callback.callbackMethod(); 
    }
}

public interface Callback {
    void callbackMethod();
}
```

Here, we have an interface called `Callback` which defines a single method `callbackMethod`. In the `Example` class, we have a method called `doSomethingWithCallback` that takes a `Callback` as an argument. Within `doSomethingWithCallback`, we do some work and then execute the `callbackMethod` on the given callback.

To use this code, we could create an implementation of `Callback` and pass it to `doSomethingWithCallback`:

```java
Example example = new Example();
example.doSomethingWithCallback(new Callback() {
    @Override
    public void callbackMethod() {
        System.out.println("Callback called");
    }
});
```

In this example, we pass an anonymous implementation of `Callback` to `doSomethingWithCallback`. When `doSomethingWithCallback` executes the `callbackMethod`, it will print out "Callback called".

This is just a simple example, but you can see how callbacks can allow for dynamic behavior within a program. Rather than hard-coding behavior into a method, you can allow callers to specify behavior via a callback.

