Here's an example of how to do this:

```
@Override
public boolean onTouch(View v, MotionEvent event) {
    switch (event.getAction()) {
        case MotionEvent.ACTION_DOWN:
            // Get finger coordinates
            float x = event.getX();
            float y = event.getY();

            // Get current scale factor
            float scale = getScale();

            // Convert finger coordinates to canvas coordinates using the scale factor
            float canvasX = x / scale;
            float canvasY = y / scale;

            // Draw circle at canvas coordinates
            circles.add(new Circle(canvasX, canvasY));
            invalidate();
            return true;
        default:
            return super.onTouchEvent(event);
    }
}

@Override
protected void onDraw(Canvas canvas) {
    super.onDraw(canvas);

    Paint paint = new Paint();
    paint.setColor(Color.RED);

    // Draw all circles
    for (Circle circle : circles) {
        float x = circle.getX() * getScale();
        float y = circle.getY() * getScale();
        canvas.drawCircle(x, y, 10, paint);
    }
}

private float getScale() {
    return getScaleX(); // or getScaleY() if you need the vertical scale factor as well
}
```

In this example, we keep a list of Circle objects and add a new Circle to the list whenever the user taps on the WebView. The Circle objects store their coordinates in terms of the canvas (not the finger coordinates), so we don't need to worry about the current scale factor when we're drawing them.

In the "onTouch" method, we convert the finger coordinates (which are in terms of the WebView) to canvas coordinates using the current scale factor. We then create a new Circle object with these canvas coordinates and add it to the list. Finally, we call "invalidate" to trigger a redraw of the canvas.

In the "onDraw" method, we iterate over all the Circle objects in the list and draw each of them using the current scale factor. Note that we use the "getX" and "getY" methods of the Circle objects to get their coordinates in terms of the canvas (not the finger coordinates).

