Yes, the JavaScript regex standard supports forcing case when doing a search/replace. 

You can use the standard modifiers "i" and "g" with the replace method to replace all occurrences of a pattern in a string with a replacement string while ignoring the case of the pattern. 

For example, if you have a string "Hello World" and you want to replace all occurrences of "world" with "there", ignoring the case of the pattern, you can use the following code:

```
var str = "Hello World";
var pattern = /world/i;
var replacement = "there";
var newStr = str.replace(pattern, replacement);
console.log(newStr); // outputs "Hello there"
```

In this code, the "i" modifier is used to ignore the case of the pattern in the regex. 

Note that if you want to replace only the first occurrence of the pattern, you can omit the "g" modifier. 

If you want to force the case of a capture group in the replacement string, you can use the $1, $2, $3... placeholders to refer to the captured groups in the regex and apply methods like toUpperCase() and toLowerCase() to them. For example:

```
var str = "Hello World";
var pattern = /(world)/i;
var replacement = "$1".toUpperCase();
var newStr = str.replace(pattern, replacement);
console.log(newStr); // outputs "Hello WORLD"
```

In this example, the captured group "world" is converted to uppercase using the toUpperCase() method and substituted back into the replacement string using the $1 placeholder.

It seems like the current approach is to spawn one task per permutation, which is not efficient and is causing the blocking issue. Instead, a better approach would be to use a thread pool to execute the DNS lookups concurrently.

One way to achieve this is to use the `rayon` library, which provides a convenient way to parallelize iterators. Here's an example implementation of the `Domain` struct's `all()` method using `rayon`:

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;
use twistrs::enrich::{DomainMetadata, Result};
use twistrs::permutate::Domain;

impl Domain {
    pub fn all_par(&self) -> Result<impl ParallelIterator<Item = String>> {
        let permutations = self.all()?;
        Ok(permutations.into_par_iter().map(|perm| {
            let metadata = DomainMetadata::new(perm.clone());
            (perm, metadata.dns_resolvable())
        }).filter_map(|(perm, dns)| {
            dns.map(|_dns| perm)
                .map_err(|error| println!("Error resolving DNS for {}: {:?}", perm, error))
                .ok()
        }))
    }
}
```

The main changes here are:

1. Using `all()` to obtain the permutations.
2. Converting the results to a parallel iterator using `into_par_iter()`.
3. Using `map()` to perform the DNS lookups for each permutation in parallel.
4. Filtering out the permutations that failed to resolve and logging an error message.
5. Returning only the successful permutations as a `ParallelIterator`.

With this implementation, we can now use `Rayon` to perform the DNS lookups concurrently. Here's an example usage:

```rust
use twistrs::permutate::Domain;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let domain = Domain::new("google.com").unwrap();
    let permutations = domain.all_par().unwrap().collect::<Vec<String>>();

    for (i, perm) in permutations.into_iter().enumerate() {
        println!("{}, {}", i, perm);
    }
}
```

This implementation should be more efficient and non-blocking, while still allowing for concurrent DNS lookups.

