The issue is that the code is using <code>setTimeout</code> with a delay of 0, which doesn't actually schedule the callback to be executed asynchronously. Instead, it gets executed on the same tick of the event loop, which means that it blocks the UI thread.

To make the loading of items asynchronous and not block the UI thread, the code should use a combination of <code>setTimeout</code> with a delay greater than 0 and <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>, which will allow the UI thread to update before more items are loaded. Here's an example:

<pre><code>var deferred = new Deferred();

// Start with a delay of 50ms
var delay = 50;

function loadNextItem(i) {
  // Load the ith item
  loadItem(items[i]);

  // Progress the deferred
  deferred.progress(i + ' - loaded');

  // Schedule the next item to be loaded
  i++;
  if (i &lt; items.length) {
    // Use requestAnimationFrame to make it asynchronous and allow the UI to update
    requestAnimationFrame(function() {
      setTimeout(function() {
        loadNextItem(i);
      }, delay);
    });
  } else {
    // All items have been loaded, resolve the deferred
    deferred.resolve();
  }
}

// Start loading the first item
loadNextItem(0);

deferred.then(function() {
  addItemsToDashboard();
}, function(err) {
  console.error('Error:', err);
}, function(progress) {
  console.debug('Update:', progress);
});
</code></pre>

This code uses a recursive approach to load each item asynchronously using <code>setTimeout</code>, with a delay that starts at 50ms and is increased by 10ms for each item loaded. It also uses <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> to allow the UI thread to update before loading the next item.

