Yes, regular expressions can be used to replace a character only if it is contained in a quoted substring. Here's an example using C#:

```csharp
string input = "axbx'cxdxe'fxgh'ixj'k";
string output = Regex.Replace(input, @"('[^']*')|x", match => {
    if (match.Value == "x" && match.Groups[1].Success) {
        return "P";
    }
    return match.Value;
});
Console.WriteLine(output);
// output: axbx'cPdPe'fxgh'iPj'k
```

The regular expression `('[^']*')|x` matches either a substring surrounded by single quotes (`'[^\']*'`) or the letter "x". The `|` operator means "or". The replacement function is called for every match and receives a `Match` object. If the match is the letter "x" and it is inside a quoted substring (which is determined by checking if the first capturing group matched), the function returns "P". Otherwise, it returns the original match value. The resulting string is the input string with all occurrences of "x" replaced with "P" inside quoted substrings.

