There are a few options for achieving this. One approach is to use a custom cell renderer for the column in question. This renderer can display a combobox with the desired options and the selected value of the combobox can be set based on the value in the cell. This approach would allow the combobox to be displayed in each row and the selected value to be shown even when the combobox is not selected.

To get rid of the change and cancel buttons, you could use a custom editor for the cell. This editor could be a combobox with the same options as the cell renderer. When the user selects a new value in the combobox, the editor can automatically update the cell value and commit the edit. This would eliminate the need for the change and cancel buttons.

Here's some example code that demonstrates these concepts:

```
import javax.swing.DefaultCellEditor;
import javax.swing.JComboBox;
import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JScrollPane;
import javax.swing.JTable;
import javax.swing.table.DefaultTableModel;
import javax.swing.table.TableCellRenderer;

public class ComboBoxTableExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // create the table model
        DefaultTableModel model = new DefaultTableModel(new Object[][]{
            {"Value 1", "ASC"},
            {"Value 2", "DEC"},
            {"Value 3", "ASC"}
        }, new String[]{"Column 1", "Column 2"});

        // create the combobox renderer and editor
        JComboBox<String> comboBox = new JComboBox<>(new String[]{"ASC", "DEC"});
        TableCellRenderer renderer = (table, value, isSelected, hasFocus, row, column) -> {
            comboBox.setSelectedItem(value);
            return comboBox;
        };
        DefaultCellEditor editor = new DefaultCellEditor(comboBox);
        editor.setClickCountToStart(1); // enable editing on single-click

        // create the table with the custom renderer and editor
        JTable table = new JTable(model) {
            @Override
            public TableCellRenderer getCellRenderer(int row, int column) {
                if (column == 1) {
                    return renderer;
                } else {
                    return super.getCellRenderer(row, column);
                }
            }

            @Override
            public DefaultCellEditor getDefaultEditor(Class<?> columnClass) {
                if (columnClass == String.class) {
                    return editor;
                } else {
                    return super.getDefaultEditor(columnClass);
                }
            }
        };

        // create the frame and add the table
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("ComboBox Table Example");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(table);
        frame.getContentPane().add(scrollPane);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }

}
```

In this example, a table model is created with two columns. The second column contains the values "ASC" and "DEC", which are the options for the combobox. The combobox itself is created as a single instance and reused for each cell so that multiple instances don't need to be created. The renderer sets the selected item of the combobox based on the cell value, and the editor commits the edit automatically when a new value is selected. The custom renderer and editor are added to the table using the `getCellRenderer` and `getDefaultEditor` methods, respectively.

