One solution is to update the cursor manually with <code>requery()</code> after modifying the database. However, <code>requery()</code> is deprecated in API level 11 (Honeycomb) and above. A better solution would be to use a <code>CursorLoader</code> with a <code>ContentProvider</code> to manage the cursor, as this automatically updates the cursor whenever there are changes to the underlying data.

To do this, you would need to create a <code>ContentProvider</code> that exposes your database data, and use a <code>CursorLoader</code> in your <code>ListActivity</code> to manage the cursor. When an item is clicked, you would update the relevant data in the database through the content provider, which would automatically update the cursor, and notify the <code>CursorLoader</code> to reload the data. This would then refresh the list view with the updated data.

Here is an example of how to use a <code>CursorLoader</code>:

<pre><code>public class MyActivity extends ListActivity implements LoaderManager.LoaderCallbacks&lt;Cursor&gt; {

    // CursorLoader ID
    private static final int LOADER_ID = 1;

    // SimpleCursorAdapter to bind data to the list view
    private SimpleCursorAdapter mAdapter;

    // ContentProvider URI
    private static final Uri CONTENT_URI = Uri.parse("content://com.example.mycontentprovider/data");

    @Override
    public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);

        // Set up the SimpleCursorAdapter and bind it to the list view
        String[] from = { "name", "value" };
        int[] to = { R.id.name_textview, R.id.value_textview };
        mAdapter = new SimpleCursorAdapter(this, R.layout.list_item, null, from, to, 0);
        setListAdapter(mAdapter);

        // Initialize the LoaderManager to manage the cursor
        getLoaderManager().initLoader(LOADER_ID, null, this);
    }

    // LoaderManager.LoaderCallbacks implementation

    @Override
    public Loader&lt;Cursor&gt; onCreateLoader(int id, Bundle args) {
        // Create a new CursorLoader with the ContentProvider URI
        return new CursorLoader(this, CONTENT_URI, null, null, null, null);
    }

    @Override
    public void onLoadFinished(Loader&lt;Cursor&gt; loader, Cursor data) {
        // Swap the new cursor into the SimpleCursorAdapter
        mAdapter.swapCursor(data);
    }

    @Override
    public void onLoaderReset(Loader&lt;Cursor&gt; loader) {
        // Release the reference to the old cursor
        mAdapter.swapCursor(null);
    }

    // Click handler for list items
    @Override
    protected void onListItemClick(ListView l, View v, int position, long id) {
        // Get the clicked item's ID
        Cursor cursor = mAdapter.getCursor();
        cursor.moveToPosition(position);
        long itemId = cursor.getLong(cursor.getColumnIndex("_id"));

        // Update the database through the ContentProvider
        ContentValues values = new ContentValues();
        values.put("flag", 1); // Or toggle the existing value, etc.
        getContentResolver().update(ContentUris.withAppendedId(CONTENT_URI, itemId), values, null, null);
    }
}</code></pre>

In this example, the <code>ContentProvider</code> would be responsible for managing the database and exposing the data through the URI <code>content://com.example.mycontentprovider/data</code>. The cursor loader would then use this URI to manage the cursor and automatically update it when there is a change. When an item is clicked, the click handler retrieves the ID of the clicked item from the cursor, updates the relevant data in the database through the content provider, and the cursor loader then reloads the data, which updates the list view with the new data.

