Here is a sample code that demonstrates how to download files asynchronously using FtpWebRequest in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string ftpHost = "ftp.example.com";
        string ftpUsername = "username";
        string ftpPassword = "password";
        string remoteFilePath = "/remote/file/path/file.txt";
        string localFilePath = "C:\\local\\file\\path\\file.txt";

        FtpWebRequest ftpWebRequest = (FtpWebRequest)WebRequest.Create($"ftp://{ftpHost}{remoteFilePath}");
        ftpWebRequest.Method = WebRequestMethods.Ftp.DownloadFile;
        ftpWebRequest.Credentials = new NetworkCredential(ftpUsername, ftpPassword);
        ftpWebRequest.UseBinary = true;

        // Enable asynchronous operation
        ftpWebRequest.BeginGetResponse((ar) =>
        {
            try
            {
                // Get the response object
                FtpWebResponse ftpWebResponse = (FtpWebResponse)ftpWebRequest.EndGetResponse(ar);

                // Get the response stream
                Stream responseStream = ftpWebResponse.GetResponseStream();

                // Create a local file stream to write the downloaded data
                FileStream fileStream = new FileStream(localFilePath, FileMode.Create);

                // Define a buffer size to read from the response stream (1KB buffer used here)
                byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];

                int bytesRead = 0;

                do
                {
                    // Read a chunk of data into the buffer
                    bytesRead = responseStream.Read(buffer, 0, buffer.Length);

                    // Write the data from the buffer to the file stream
                    fileStream.Write(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
                } while (bytesRead > 0);

                // Close the streams
                fileStream.Close();
                responseStream.Close();

                Console.WriteLine($"File downloaded successfully to {localFilePath}");
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Error while downloading the file: {ex.Message}");
            }
        }, null);

        Console.ReadKey();
    }
}
```

In this code, we first define the FTP server host, username, and password, as well as the remote and local file paths. We then create an instance of the `FtpWebRequest` class and set its properties, including the `Method` property which is set to `DownloadFile`, and the `Credentials` property which is set to the FTP server's username and password. We also enable binary mode for the transfer using the `UseBinary` property.

Next, we enable asynchronous operation using the `BeginGetResponse` method, which starts the download and returns immediately, allowing the main thread to continue executing. The `EndGetResponse` method is called in the async callback to retrieve the response object.

We then get the response stream and create a local file stream to write the downloaded data. We define a buffer size of 1KB to read from the response stream, and we then read data from the response stream into the buffer and write it to the local file stream. We repeat this process until all data has been read.

Finally, we close the streams and output a message indicating that the file has been downloaded successfully.

Note that this code uses a single connection to the FTP server for all downloads, and the connection is automatically added to the connection pool. If you want to use a specific connection group, you can set the `ConnectionGroupName` property of the `FtpWebRequest` object.

