You are on the right track using the Euler method to simulate the motion of your object. However, you are not correctly applying the concept of conservation of energy during the collision with the ground.

When an object collides with the ground, some of its kinetic energy is transferred to other forms such as heat or deformation of the object. This means that the total energy of the system (your object + the Earth) is not conserved, but that energy is lost. This is what causes the object to eventually come to a stop.

To simulate this in your program, you need to apply a coefficient of restitution. This coefficient represents the ratio of the final velocity to the initial velocity after a collision. A value of 1 means that the collision is perfectly elastic, and the object will bounce back with the same velocity it had before the collision. A value between 0 and 1 means that the collision is partially elastic, and some energy is lost. A value of 0 means that the collision is completely inelastic, and the object will stick to the ground.

In your code, you are multiplying the velocity by the energy loss factor, but you are not taking into account the fact that the object is bouncing back. Instead of multiplying the velocity by the energy loss factor, you should multiply it by the coefficient of restitution, and then invert the direction of the velocity (since the object is bouncing back). Here is an updated version of your code:

