The reason for this behavior is that when you define an observable as an object, Knockout automatically creates a computed observable with read/write access to each property of the object. This is known as "observable object" in Knockout.

So when you assign `model.thing` as an observable, and then add the `someProp` property to it, Knockout creates a dependent observable that automatically tracks changes to `model.thing.someProp`. This is why you see it behaving as a computed.

On the other hand, when you assign `model.thing` as a simple object and pass it in as a parameter to the component, it does not become an observable object, so it does not have this behavior.

If you want to avoid this behavior, you can either define `model.thing` as a simple object from the start, or you can use the `ko.toJS()` utility function to convert it to a simple object before passing it into the component. 

Here's an example of how you could use `ko.toJS()` to pass in `model.thing` as a simple object:

```
ko.components.register('custom-element', {
  viewModel: function(params) {
    this.value = params.value;
    alert(this.value); // now this is not dependent
  }, 
  template:
  '<div data-bind="text: value"></div>'
});

var viewModel = function() {
  var model = {};
  model.thing = ko.observable({ someProp: "Yolo" });
  // Convert model.thing to a simple object using ko.toJS
  model.thing = ko.toJS(model.thing);
  return model;
};

ko.applyBindings(viewModel);
```

