To speed up reading the file into a byte array, you can try using a BufferedStream instead of a FileStream. BufferedStream uses an internal buffer to reduce the number of reads from the underlying stream.

You can also adjust the buffer size to find the optimal size for your specific use case. A buffer size of 102400 bytes may be too large or too small depending on the file size and system resources.

To create multiple byte arrays based on offsets containing a specific byte sequence, you can use the Array.Copy method to copy the bytes from the input buffer to a new output buffer. Here is an example code snippet:

byte[] searchPattern = { 0b00110011 };
int searchOffset = 100;
int bufferOffset = 0;
int bytesRead;
do
{
    bytesRead = bufferedStream.Read(buf, bufferOffset, buf.Length - bufferOffset);

    for (int i = 0; i < bytesRead; i++)
    {
        if (buf[i] == searchPattern[0] && i + searchPattern.Length < bytesRead)
        {
            bool found = true;
            for (int j = 1; j < searchPattern.Length; j++)
            {
                if (buf[i + j] != searchPattern[j])
                {
                    found = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (found)
            {
                int copyLength = i - bufferOffset + searchPattern.Length;
                byte[] copyBuffer = new byte[copyLength];
                Array.Copy(buf, bufferOffset, copyBuffer, 0, copyLength);
                // do something with the copyBuffer
            }
        }
    }
    bufferOffset += bytesRead;
} while (bytesRead > 0);

Note that this code is simplified and assumes that the search pattern is always one byte long. You would need to modify it to handle multi-byte search patterns. Also, keep in mind that allocating multiple byte arrays can be memory-intensive if the file is very large.

