To handle input types containing interface properties in HotChocolate library, you need to configure a custom type converter for the interface type. Here's an example of how to do it:

1. Define a custom type converter for the interface type:

```
public class LocationFilterTypeConverter : IInputTypeConverter
{
    public bool CanConvert(ITypeConversionContext context)
    {
        return typeof(ILocationFilter).IsAssignableFrom(context.Target.GetType());
    }

    public object Convert(ITypeConversionContext context)
    {
        var input = context.Input<ILocationFilter>();
        var type = typeof(LocationFilterInputType<>).MakeGenericType(input.GetType());
        return Activator.CreateInstance(type);
    }
}
```

2. Define an input type for the interface type:

```
public class LocationFilterInputType<T> : InputObjectType<T> where T : ILocationFilter
{
    protected override void Configure(IInputObjectTypeDescriptor<T> descriptor)
    {
        descriptor.Name("LocationFilter");
        descriptor.Field(f => f.Lat).Type<NonNullType<FloatType>>();
        descriptor.Field(f => f.Lng).Type<NonNullType<FloatType>>();
    }
}
```

3. Register the type converter and the input type in your schema:

```
services.AddGraphQL(SchemaBuilder.New()
                        .AddQueryType<Query>()
                        .AddTypeConverter(new LocationFilterTypeConverter())
                        .AddType<LocationFilterInputType<LocationFilter>>()
                        .Create(),
                    new QueryExecutionOptions { IncludeExceptionDetails = true });
```

With these changes, you should be able to use the FilterModel input type with the Location property containing any implementation of the ILocationFilter interface.

