2. Modify your code as follows:

```
camelContext.addRoutes(new RouteBuilder() {
    public void configure() {
        onException(Exception.class)
        .to("log:GeneralError?level=ERROR")
        .end();

        from("jms:queue:TestQueue?concurrentConsumers=1")
        .marshal(xmlJsonFormat)
        .to("file:/tmp/messages?fileName=report.txt&amp;fileExist=Append")
        .to("stream:out")
        .to("cxf:http://localhost:9080/WebContext/TestResource"
            + "?dataFormat=PAYLOAD"
            + "&headerFilterStrategy=#dropHeadersStrategy"
            + "&throwExceptionOnFailure=false")
        ;
    }
});

// Add a custom header filter strategy bean to drop unwanted headers
// This bean allows you to define a list of headers to drop from the CXF message
camelContext.getRegistry().bind("dropHeadersStrategy", new HeaderFilterStrategy() {
    List<String> droppedHeaders = Arrays.asList(
        "Content-Length",
        "SOAPAction",
        "Accept-Encoding"
    );

    @Override
    public boolean applyFilterToCamelHeaders(String headerName, Object headerValue, Exchange exchange) {
        return droppedHeaders.contains(headerName);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean applyFilterToExternalHeaders(String headerName, Object headerValue, Exchange exchange) {
        return droppedHeaders.contains(headerName);
    }
});
```

3. In the above code, `cxf:http://localhost:9080/WebContext/TestResource` specifies the REST endpoint URL that you want to post to. `dataFormat=PAYLOAD` specifies that the CXF component will read and write message payloads only. `headerFilterStrategy=#dropHeadersStrategy` specifies the custom header filter strategy bean defined earlier to remove unwanted headers. `throwExceptionOnFailure=false` specifies that if the REST call fails, an exception will not be thrown.

