As an AI language model, I cannot say which MySQL version you are using, but assuming you are using a version of MySQL that does not have the Title Case function, you can create a custom function to achieve the desired effect. Here is an example implementation of a custom Title Case function in MySQL:

First, you can create a function that takes in a string and returns the Title Case version:

```
DELIMITER $$

CREATE FUNCTION title_case(str VARCHAR(255))
RETURNS VARCHAR(255)
BEGIN
  DECLARE i INT DEFAULT 1;
  DECLARE j INT;
  DECLARE words VARCHAR(255);
  DECLARE final_str VARCHAR(255);

  SET str = LOWER(str);
  SET final_str = UPPER(SUBSTRING(str, 1, 1));

  WHILE i <= LENGTH(str) DO
    IF SUBSTRING(str, i, 1) = ' ' THEN
      SET final_str = CONCAT(final_str, ' ');
      SET i = i + 1;
    ELSE
      SET j = LOCATE(' ', str, i);
      IF j = 0 THEN
        SET words = SUBSTRING(str, i);
      ELSE
        SET words = SUBSTRING(str, i, j - i);
      END IF;

      IF NOT FIND_IN_SET(words, 'a,an,and,the,as,at,but,by,en,for,if,in,of,on,or,to,v,vs') THEN
        SET final_str = CONCAT(final_str, UPPER(SUBSTRING(words, 1, 1)), LOWER(SUBSTRING(words, 2)));
      ELSE
        SET final_str = CONCAT(final_str, words);
      END IF;

      SET i = j + 1;
    END IF;
  END WHILE;

  RETURN final_str;
END$$

DELIMITER ;
```

With this function, you can then update your table to convert the column values to Title Case:

```
UPDATE your_table SET your_column = title_case(your_column);
``` 

Please note that the `title_case` function is not perfect and may not handle all cases of "small words" correctly, but it should suffice for most use cases.

