<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      size: window.innerWidth,
    };
  },
  mounted() {
    // Call the `updateSize` method whenever the window is resized
    window.addEventListener('resize', this.updateSize);
  },
  beforeDestroy() {
    // Remove the event listener when the component is destroyed
    window.removeEventListener('resize', this.updateSize);
  },
  methods: {
    updateSize() {
      this.size = window.innerWidth;
    },
  },
  computed: {
    // Define the component or template to render based on screen size
    smallScreenComponent() {
      return 'SmallScreenComponent';
    },
    largeScreenComponent() {
      return 'LargeScreenComponent';
    },
  },
};
</script>
```

In this example, the `size` data property is initially set to the width of the window using the `window.innerWidth` property. The `updateSize` method is called whenever the window is resized, and it sets the `size` property to the new width of the window.

The `mounted` lifecycle hook adds an event listener to the `resize` event using the `window.addEventListener` method, and passes the `updateSize` method as the event handler.

The `beforeDestroy` lifecycle hook is used to remove the event listener when the component is destroyed, using the `window.removeEventListener` method.

The `computed` properties `smallScreenComponent` and `largeScreenComponent` return the names of the components (or templates) to render based on the value of the `size` property. In this example, the `SmallScreenComponent` is rendered for window widths less than `767px`, and the `LargeScreenComponent` is rendered for window widths greater than or equal to `767px`.

