It seems like you might have a problem with the pagination number being updated wrongly. One solution could be to use a custom ItemReader that handles pagination correctly. You can implement the PagingAndSortingRepository from Spring Data JPA to create a custom reader that reads by using the correct offset and page size. This would ensure that the pagination number is updated correctly and you don't miss any items from the table. 

Here's a sample implementation of a custom reader:

<pre><code>@Component
@StepScope
public class FooPagingReader implements ItemReader&lt;Foo&gt; {

    private PagingAndSortingRepository&lt;Foo, Long&gt; fooRepository;

    private int pageSize;

    private int currentPage = 0;

    private long currentOffset = 0L;

    public FooPagingReader(PagingAndSortingRepository&lt;Foo, Long&gt; fooRepository, int pageSize) {
        this.fooRepository = fooRepository;
        this.pageSize = pageSize;
    }

    @Override
    public Foo read() {
        Pageable pageable = PageRequest.of(currentPage++, pageSize);
        Page&lt;Foo&gt; fooPage = fooRepository.findAll(pageable);
        if (currentPage &gt;= fooPage.getTotalPages()) {
            return null; // no more pages
        }
        currentOffset += pageSize;
        return fooPage.getContent().get(0);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Note that this implementation assumes that your Foo entity has a Long id field. You can adjust this implementation as needed to match your entity and repository. Also, keep in mind that this implementation reads one item at a time, so if you're writing to the same table, you might need to adjust your transaction size accordingly to avoid deadlocks.

