What are some common best practices for custom exception class design?</s> 
Here are some common best practices for custom exception class design:

1. Use descriptive names for your exception classes
   Your custom exception classes should have descriptive names that clearly indicate what the exception represents. For example, an exception class named "InvalidInputException" makes it clear that the exception is thrown when invalid input is received.

2. Extend the appropriate exception class
   Your custom exception class should extend the appropriate exception class. For example, if your exception represents an error in input validation, it should extend the "IllegalArgumentException" class.

3. Provide a useful message
   Your custom exception should provide a message that is clear, concise, and useful for debugging. The message should explain what went wrong and possibly provide additional information that can help the programmer or user debug the problem.

4. Include a cause or stack trace
   If your exception is caused by another exception or error, you should include the cause or stack trace in your exception. This information can help the programmer or user understand the source of the problem and how to fix it.

5. Use checked exceptions sparingly
   Checked exceptions should be used sparingly, as they can make code more complex and harder to read. Consider using unchecked exceptions for most cases, and only use checked exceptions when it is necessary for the caller to handle the exception.

6. Document the exceptions that your method can throw
   If your method can throw exceptions, you should document them using the @throws annotation in the method's Javadoc. This makes it easier for other programmers to understand the method's behavior and to handle exceptions appropriately.

