One possible pattern that will capture the name and selected value (if any) is:

<pre><code>$pattern = '/&lt;select name="([a-zA-Z]*)".*?&lt;option value="(NONE|[A-Z]{2,4})"( selected="selected")?.*?&lt;\/select&gt;/s'</code></pre>

This pattern will:

- Capture the name attribute of the select tag using the regular expression notation ([a-zA-Z]*).
- Capture the value attribute of the option tag, which can be "NONE" or a two to four uppercase letter country code ([A-Z]{2,4}).
- Optionally capture the selected attribute of the option tag using the ( selected="selected")? group. This group will only match if the selected attribute is present.
- Use the /s modifier at the end to make the dot (.) character match newlines as well. This is necessary to match multi-line HTML code.

You can use this pattern in PHP like so:

<pre><code>$matches = [];
if (preg_match($pattern, $page, $matches)) {
    $name = $matches[1];
    $value = isset($matches[3]) ? $matches[2] : 'NONE';
    echo "Name: $name, Value: $value";
} else {
    echo "No match found";
}</code></pre>

This code will output "Name: country, Value: US" for the provided $page example. If the "selected" attribute were not present, it would output "Name: country, Value: NONE".

The issue seems to be with the `id` passed to the `Error` component. You're currently passing the index of the error in the `errors` array as the `id`. However, when you remove an error from the array, the indices of the remaining errors change, but the `id` you assigned to them remains the same.

One solution would be to generate a unique `id` for each error object when it's added to the array, instead of using the index as the `id`. You can use a library like `uuid` to generate unique ids.

Add the following line to your project to install the `uuid` library:

```
npm install uuid
```

Then, import it in your `Errors.jsx` component:

```js
import { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';
```

When you add a new error to the array, generate a unique `id` using `uuidv4()` and use it to create a new error object:

```js
setSiteSettings(site => ({
  ...site,
  errors: [
    ...site.errors,
    { message: 'Some error', id: uuidv4() } // generate a unique id
  ]
}));
```

Now, your `Error` component will have a unique `id` for each error object, and you can safely filter them out without worrying about changing indices.

Here's the updated `Error` component code:

```js
import { useContext, useEffect } from 'react';
import { SiteContext } from '../context/SiteContext';
import { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';

const Error = props => {
  const [siteSettings, setSiteSettings] = useContext(SiteContext);
  // find the error with the matching id
  const filteredAry = siteSettings.errors.filter(obj => obj.id !== props.id);
  // after 3 seconds, update the array without the error with the matching id
  useEffect(() => {
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
      setSiteSettings(siteSettings => ({
        ...siteSettings,
        errors: filteredAry
      }));
    }, 3000);
    return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId); // clear the timeout on unmount
  }, []);

  return <div className="error">{props.error}</div>;
};

const Errors = () => {
  const [site, setSiteSettings] = useContext(SiteContext);
  const addError = () => {
    setSiteSettings(site => ({
      ...site,
      errors: [
        ...site.errors,
        { message: 'Some error', id: uuidv4() } // generate a unique id
      ]
    }));
  };
  return (
    <div id="errors">
      Error page
      {site.errors.map(error => (
        <Error key={error.id} id={error.id} error={error.message} />
      ))}
      <button onClick={addError}>Add error</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Errors;
``` 

Note that the `key` prop is now set to the `id` of the error object, and is required when rendering a list of components in React.

