Unfortunately, it is not possible to return the intermediate results of a function in SQL while it is still running. SQL is a declarative language, which means that it specifies what to compute and not how to compute it. This is in contrast to procedural languages like Java or Python, where you can write a loop that prints out intermediate results.

The best you can do is to refactor your function to return all the results at once. This can be achieved by creating an array in the function and returning it at the end. For example:

<pre><code>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_all_foo() RETURNS int[] AS
$BODY$
DECLARE
    i int;
    results int[] := '{}';
begin
    i=1;
    LOOP
        results := array_append(results, f(i));
        i=i+1;
        EXIT when i&gt;1000;
    END LOOP;
    RETURN results;
END
$BODY$
LANGUAGE plpgsql;
</code></pre>

This function will return an array of all the results of f(1) through f(1000), which you can then iterate over and print out the results one by one. This may not be exactly what you were hoping for, but unfortunately, it is the best you can do in SQL.

