Yes, there is a better way to achieve this. You can use a recursive function with the help of the <code>$children</code> property to traverse the component tree only once and collect all the desired inputs. Here's an example implementation:

<strong>app-form</strong>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;template&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
    &lt;slot&gt;&lt;/slot&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export default {
  props: {
    value: Boolean
  },
  data() {
    return {
      validations: [] // Array of validation results for each input
    };
  },
  watch: {
    validations: function() {
      // Emit the new validation results to the parent component
      const isValid = this.validations.every(v => v);
      this.$emit("input", isValid);
    }
  },
  mounted() {
    // Collect all the app-input components in the form and register their validations
    this.collectInputs(this);
  },
  methods: {
    collectInputs(node) {
      node.$children.forEach(child => {
        if (child.$options.name === "app-input") {
          this.validations.push(false);
          // Register a callback to update the validation status of this input
          child.$on("validation", valid => {
            const index = this.validations.findIndex(v => v === false);
            this.validations[index] = valid;
          });
        } else {
          this.collectInputs(child);
        }
      });
    }
  }
};
</code></pre>

<strong>app-input</strong>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;template&gt;
  &lt;input :value="value" @input="onInput" /&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export default {
  props: {
    value: String,
    rules: { type: Array, default: () => [] }
  },
  data() {
    return {
      valid: false // The validation status of this input
    };
  },
  methods: {
    validate() {
      const valid = this.rules.every(rule => rule(this.value));
      this.valid = valid;
      this.$emit("validation", valid);
    },
    onInput(event) {
      this.$emit("input", event.target.value);
    }
  },
  watch: {
    value() {
      this.validate();
    }
  }
};
</code></pre>

With this approach, each input component emits a "validation" event whenever its validation status changes, and the form component updates its overall validation status whenever any of these events are received. This way, the form only needs to traverse the component tree once during initialization, and each input component only needs to validate itself when its value changes.

