In Qt 5, the QDialogButtonBox class no longer retrieves translations from the corresponding qt_ts.qm file. Instead, it uses the platform's native dialog button strings.

To work around this issue and retrieve the translated strings in qt_xx.qm, you can create a subclass of QDialogButtonBox and override its translation function to retrieve the translated strings from the loaded translator. Here's an example:

```
class TranslatableButtonBox : public QDialogButtonBox {
public:
    TranslatableButtonBox(QWidget *parent = nullptr) :
        QDialogButtonBox(parent)
    {}

    void changeEvent(QEvent *event) override {
        if (event->type() == QEvent::LanguageChange) {
            QTranslator *t = qApp->property("qtTranslator").value<QTranslator*>();
            if (t) {
                setStandardButtons(QDialogButtonBox::Cancel |
                                    QDialogButtonBox::Ok |
                                    QDialogButtonBox::Save |
                                    QDialogButtonBox::Open |
                                    QDialogButtonBox::Yes |
                                    QDialogButtonBox::No);
                button(QDialogButtonBox::Cancel)->setText(tr("Cancel"));
                button(QDialogButtonBox::Ok)->setText(tr("OK"));
                button(QDialogButtonBox::Save)->setText(tr("Save"));
                button(QDialogButtonBox::Open)->setText(tr("Open"));
                button(QDialogButtonBox::Yes)->setText(tr("Yes"));
                button(QDialogButtonBox::No)->setText(tr("No"));
                return;
            }
        }
        QDialogButtonBox::changeEvent(event);
    }
};
```

This subclass provides a custom implementation of the `changeEvent()` function to handle language changes. It first checks if a Qt translator has been loaded and retrieves it using the `qtTranslator` property of the `QApplication` instance. If a translator has been loaded, it sets the standard buttons of the button box to the expected values and retrieves the translated strings using the `tr()` function.

To use this subclass instead of the standard `QDialogButtonBox`, you can modify your `showMessage()` function as follows:

```
void MainWindow::showMessage()
