Yes, there is a function that can compare two string vectors and return the number of different (or the same) elements. It is called std::mismatch() and is part of the <algorithm> header in C++. 

Here's an example code that demonstrates the usage of std::mismatch():

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> s1{"red", "green", "blue"};
    std::vector<std::string> s2{"red", "orange", "blue"};

    auto result = std::mismatch(s1.begin(), s1.end(), s2.begin());

    if (result.first == s1.end() && result.second == s2.end()) {
        std::cout << "Vectors are the same\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Vectors are different at position "
                  << std::distance(s1.begin(), result.first) << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, s1 and s2 are two string vectors to be compared. The std::mismatch() function is passed the iterators of both vectors as arguments. It returns a pair of iterators pointing to the first elements that are not equal in both containers.

If both vectors have the same elements, then the returned iterators will be equal to their respective vectors end iterators. If the vectors are different, then the distance between the first iterator and the beginning of the vector will give you the index of the first different element.

Note that std::mismatch() only compares the elements pairwise and stops as soon as the first difference is found. If you want to count all the different elements or compare the vectors regardless of the order, you will need to iterate over both vectors and compare each element individually.

