The issue with the code is that the bitwise and operator (&) is being used instead of the right-shift operator (>>). 

When a color value is bitwise ANDed with 0xFF0000, 0x00FF00, or 0x0000FF, it only keeps the bits that pertain to that color channel and discards the rest, resulting in a value that is likely much smaller than the original color value. 

To fix this, replace the bitwise and (&) with a right-shift (>>) like so:

<pre><code>function interpolateColor($corA, $corB, $lerp)
{
    $redA = ($corA >> 16) &amp; 0xFF;
    $greenA = ($corA >> 8) &amp; 0xFF;
    $blueA = $corA &amp; 0xFF;
    $redB = ($corB >> 16) &amp; 0xFF;
    $greenB = ($corB >> 8) &amp; 0xFF;
    $blueB = $corB &amp; 0xFF;

    $redC = $redA + (($redB - $redA) * $lerp);
    $greenC = $greenA + (($greenB - $greenA) * $lerp);
    $blueC = $blueA + (($blueB - $blueA) * $lerp);

    $color = ($redC << 16) | ($greenC << 8) | $blueC;

    return $color;
}
</code></pre>

This code correctly extracts the red, green, and blue components of the color values and then combines them back into a single color value for interpolation.

One way to make the script more efficient is to use the requestAnimationFrame method instead of the scroll event. This method allows the browser to schedule the animation updates in sync with the page's rendering cycle, resulting in a smoother and more performant animation.

Here's an updated script using requestAnimationFrame:

```
$(document).ready(function() {
  var icon = $("#icon");
  var iconHeight = icon.height();
  var textOffset = $("#text").offset().top;

  function updateIconOpacity() {
    var scrollTop = $(window).scrollTop();
    var delta = Math.max(textOffset - scrollTop + iconHeight, 0) / iconHeight;
    icon.css({ opacity: delta });
  }

  $(window).on("scroll", function() {
    requestAnimationFrame(updateIconOpacity);
  });

  updateIconOpacity();
});
```

The script calculates the opacity of the icon based on its position relative to the text content, which is retrieved once on page load. The icon's opacity is updated only when the browser is ready to render the frame using requestAnimationFrame.

Note that requestAnimationFrame is not supported in some older browsers, so you may want to include a fallback for those cases.

To add a marker on an imageView, you can create a custom Drawable class and override the draw method to draw the marker. Here is an example:

First, create a new class "MarkerDrawable" that extends "Drawable":

<pre><code>public class MarkerDrawable extends Drawable {

    private Bitmap markerBitmap;
    private Paint paint;

    public MarkerDrawable(Bitmap markerBitmap) {
        this.markerBitmap = markerBitmap;
        paint = new Paint();
        paint.setColor(Color.RED);
        paint.setStyle(Paint.Style.FILL);
    }

    @Override
    public void draw(Canvas canvas) {
        // Draw the marker bitmap at the center of the canvas
        int left = (canvas.getWidth() - markerBitmap.getWidth()) / 2;
        int top = (canvas.getHeight() - markerBitmap.getHeight()) / 2;
        canvas.drawBitmap(markerBitmap, left, top, null);

        // Draw a circle at the center of the marker bitmap
        canvas.drawCircle(canvas.getWidth() / 2, canvas.getHeight() / 2, 5, paint);
    }

    @Override
    public void setAlpha(int alpha) {
        // This method is required by the Drawable class but we do not need it
    }

    @Override
    public void setColorFilter(ColorFilter colorFilter) {
        // This method is required by the Drawable class but we do not need it
    }

    @Override
    public int getOpacity() {
        // This method is required by the Drawable class but we do not need it
        return PixelFormat.TRANSPARENT;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Next, in your customImageView's "onDraw" method, create an instance of the MarkerDrawable class and draw it on the canvas:

<pre><code>@Override
protected void onDraw(Canvas canvas) {
    super.onDraw(canvas);

    // Create a marker drawable with a bitmap
    Bitmap markerBitmap = BitmapFactory.decodeResource(getResources(), R.drawable.marker);
    MarkerDrawable markerDrawable = new MarkerDrawable(markerBitmap);

    // Set the size of the marker drawable
    markerDrawable.setBounds(0, 0, markerBitmap.getWidth(), markerBitmap.getHeight());

    // Draw the marker drawable at the center of the canvas
    int left = (canvas.getWidth() - markerDrawable.getIntrinsicWidth()) / 2;
    int top = (canvas.getHeight() - markerDrawable.getIntrinsicHeight()) / 2;
    canvas.translate(left, top);
    markerDrawable.draw(canvas);
}
</code></pre>

