One approach to parse this config file in a more structured and clean way is to use a recursive descent parser. This type of parser is well-suited for parsing nested structures, like the ones you have in your config file. 

Here's a basic example of how you can implement a recursive descent parser for your config file:

1. Start with a top-level parse method that reads the file line by line and initiates parsing of each section:
```
void parse(String filePath) {
  try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(filePath)))) {

    String line = reader.readLine();
    while (line != null) {
      switch (line) {
        case "[users]":
          parseUsers(reader, new HashMap<String, Object>());
          break;
        case "[backgrounds]":
          parseBackgrounds(reader, new HashMap<String, Object>());
          break;
        default:
          // handle unrecognized sections
          break;
      }
      line = reader.readLine();
    }

  } catch (IOException e) {
    // handle file I/O errors
  }
}
```

2. Implement separate parse methods for each section type (users, backgrounds, etc.) that take a `BufferedReader` object and a `Map` object to hold the parsed key-value pairs:
```
void parseUsers(BufferedReader reader, Map<String, Object> result) throws IOException {
  String line = reader.readLine();
  while (line != null && !line.startsWith("[")) {
    String[] tokens = line.split(":");
    if (tokens.length == 2) {
      result.put(tokens[0].trim(), parseValue(tokens[1].trim()));
    }
    line = reader.readLine();
  }

  // handle nested sections recursively
  if (line != null && line.startsWith("[")) {
    reader.reset();
    parseNestedSection(reader, "attributes", result);
  }
}

void parseNestedSection(BufferedReader reader, String sectionName, Map<String, Object> parentResult) throws IOException {
  Map<String, Object> nestedResult = new HashMap<>();
  String line = reader.readLine();
  while (line != null && !line.startsWith("[")) {
    if (line.startsWith("\t")) {
      String[] tokens = line.trim().split(":");
      if (tokens.length == 2) {
        nestedResult.put(tokens[0].trim(), parseValue(tokens[1].trim()));
      }
    }
    line = reader.readLine();
  }

  if (!nestedResult.isEmpty()) {
    parentResult.put(sectionName, nestedResult);
  }

  // handle multiple nested sections recursively
  if (line != null && line.startsWith("[")) {
    reader.reset();
    parseNestedSection(reader, "another_section", parentResult);
  }
}
```

3. Implement a method to parse individual values, depending on their type (e.g. string, integer, boolean, etc.):
```
Object parseValue(String value) {
  if (value.startsWith("\"") && value.endsWith("\"")) {
    return value.substring(1, value.length() - 1);
  } else if (value.equalsIgnoreCase("true") || value.equalsIgnoreCase("false")) {
    return Boolean.valueOf(value);
  } else if (value.matches("\\d+")) {
    return Integer.valueOf(value);
  } else {
    return value;
  }
}
```

This is just a basic example, and you'll likely need to adapt it to your specific needs. However, a recursive descent parser can provide a more structured and readable approach to parsing nested config files, compared to using a series of if-else statements and while loops.

