If you encounter the java.lang.IllegalStateException error, it could be due to the fact that the response has already been committed. To resolve this, you can try wrapping the response using a SaveContextOnUpdateOrErrorResponseWrapper. Here's an updated version of the logout success handler that adds this wrapper:

```
import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.logout.SimpleUrlLogoutSuccessHandler;
import org.springframework.security.web.context.SaveContextOnUpdateOrErrorResponseWrapper;
import com.dc.api.model.Users;

public class DCSimpleUrlLogoutSuccessHandler extends SimpleUrlLogoutSuccessHandler {

  @Override
  public void onLogoutSuccess(HttpServletRequest request, 
                              HttpServletResponse response,
                              Authentication authentication)
      throws java.io.IOException, javax.servlet.ServletException {

    Users user = null;
    Object principal = authentication.getPrincipal();

    if (principal instanceof Users) {
      user = (Users) principal;

      if (user.getType().equals("TEST")) {
        response = new SaveContextOnUpdateOrErrorResponseWrapper(response);
        response.sendRedirect("https://example.com/logout");
      }
    }

    response = new SaveContextOnUpdateOrErrorResponseWrapper(response);
    response.sendRedirect("login.html");
  }
}
``` 

The SaveContextOnUpdateOrErrorResponseWrapper will save the response results in case an exception occurs.

