In your `QuickSort` method, you are checking if `q < 1` instead of `q < p`. This might cause the partitioning to go out of bounds causing the `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundException`. Also, the line `swap(A, p, rand.nextInt() % (q+1));` might also cause issues because the random integer generated might be negative causing the index `p` to go out of bounds. You can modify it to `swap(A, p, rand.nextInt(q - p + 1) + p);` to generate only positive indices between `p` and `q`.

Here's the corrected code:

```
public class JavaQuickSort {

    public static void QuickSort(int A[], int p, int q) {
        int i, last = p;

        Random rand = new Random();
        if (q < p) {
            return;
        }
        swap(A, p, rand.nextInt(q - p + 1) + p);

        for (i = p + 1; i <= q; i++) {
            if (A[i] < A[p]) {
                swap(A, ++last, i);
            }

        }
        swap(A, p, last);
        QuickSort(A, p, last - 1);
        QuickSort(A, last + 1, q);
    }

    private static void swap(int[] A, int i, int j) {
        int temp;
        temp = A[i];
        A[i] = A[j];
        A[j] = temp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] A = {2, 5, 7, 3, 9, 0, 1, 6, 8};
        QuickSort(A, 0, 8);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(A));
    }
}
```

