Yes, you can get a list of all connected clients by keeping track of them as they connect to the server. Here is an example in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;

class Server
{
    static TcpListener listener;
    static List<TcpClient> clients = new List<TcpClient>();

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        try
        {
            listener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Any, 12345);
            listener.Start();
            Console.WriteLine("Server started");

            while (true)
            {
                TcpClient client = listener.AcceptTcpClient();
                clients.Add(client);
                Console.WriteLine("Client connected");

                // handle the client in a separate thread, e.g.
                // new Thread(HandleClient).Start(client);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
        }
    }

    static void HandleClient(object obj)
    {
        TcpClient client = (TcpClient) obj;

        // handle the client here
    }

    static void DisconnectClient(TcpClient client)
    {
        client.Close();
        clients.Remove(client);
        Console.WriteLine("Client disconnected");
    }

    static void PrintConnectedClients()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Connected clients:");

        foreach (TcpClient client in clients)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(((IPEndPoint)client.Client.RemoteEndPoint).Address);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we keep track of all connected clients in a `List<TcpClient> clients`. Whenever a new client connects, we add it to the list. We can then print out the list of connected clients using the `PrintConnectedClients` method. When a client disconnects, we remove it from the list.

