Yes, it is possible to set a different image to each face of the cube. The approach to do this would be to create a new texture for each face and map it to the corresponding vertices of that face. Here is some sample code to get you started:

<pre><code>public void drawCube(GL gl) {
    // define the vertices of the cube
    float[][] vertices = {...};

    // define the texture coordinates for each face
    float[][] texCoords = {...};

    // define the indices of each face
    int[][] indices = {...};

    // enable texture mapping
    gl.glEnable(GL.GL_TEXTURE_2D);

    // loop through each face
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; indices.length; i++) {
        // bind the texture for this face
        Texture texture = Textures.get(i);
        texture.bind();

        // define the vertex and texture coordinates for this face
        float[] faceVertices = new float[indices[i].length * 3];
        float[] faceTexCoords = new float[indices[i].length * 2];
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; indices[i].length; j++) {
            int index = indices[i][j];
            faceVertices[j * 3] = vertices[index][0];
            faceVertices[j * 3 + 1] = vertices[index][1];
            faceVertices[j * 3 + 2] = vertices[index][2];
            faceTexCoords[j * 2] = texCoords[i][j * 2];
            faceTexCoords[j * 2 + 1] = texCoords[i][j * 2 + 1];
        }

        // enable vertex and texture coordinate arrays
        gl.glEnableClientState(GL.GL_VERTEX_ARRAY);
        gl.glEnableClientState(GL.GL_TEXTURE_COORD_ARRAY);

        // specify the vertex and texture coordinate arrays
        gl.glVertexPointer(3, GL.GL_FLOAT, 0, FloatBuffer.wrap(faceVertices));
        gl.glTexCoordPointer(2, GL.GL_FLOAT, 0, FloatBuffer.wrap(faceTexCoords));

        // draw the face
        gl.glDrawArrays(GL.GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP, 0, indices[i].length);

        // disable arrays
        gl.glDisableClientState(GL.GL_VERTEX_ARRAY);
        gl.glDisableClientState(GL.GL_TEXTURE_COORD_ARRAY);
    }
}</code></pre>

In this code, the vertices, texture coordinates, and indices are defined for each face of the cube. The Textures.get(i) method is assumed to return the texture for the ith face of the cube. The loop through each face binds the appropriate texture and specifies the vertex and texture coordinate arrays for that face. The glDrawArrays method is then used to draw the face.

