As you have correctly identified, reading a large file into a single array can cause memory issues. Therefore, it's best to read the file in chunks or blocks.

Here's an updated program that reads a file in blocks and calculates its SHA-256 hash value:

```
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class ToHash {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, IOException {

        // The name of the file to read.
        String fileName = "C:\\Users\\ghasemi\\Desktop\\1.png";

        // Create a MessageDigest instance for SHA-256.
        MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");

        // Set the block size to 1 MB.
        // You can adjust this value according to your needs.
        int blockSize = 1024 * 1024;

        // Open the file for reading.
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(new File(fileName));

        // Create a buffer to read the file in blocks.
        byte[] buffer = new byte[blockSize];

        // Read the file in blocks.
        int bytesRead;
        while ((bytesRead = fis.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            // Update the digest with the current block.
            digest.update(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
        }

        // Close the file input stream.
        fis.close();

        // Get the hash value as a byte array.
        byte[] hash = digest.digest();

        // Print the hash value as a base64 encoded string.
        System.out.println(new BASE64Encoder().encode(hash));
    }
}
```

