There are several ways to read the contents of a BLOB column in Oracle:

1. Using SQL Developer: If you are using SQL Developer, you can right-click on the BLOB column in the query result and select "Export LOB" to save the contents to a file.

2. Using SQL*Plus: If you are using SQL*Plus, you can use the following SQL statement to read the contents of a BLOB column:

   SELECT TO_LOB(blob_column) FROM table_name WHERE <condition>;

   This will return the BLOB value in its hex representation. You can then convert it to a readable format using a BLOB decoding tool or a programming language that supports BLOB manipulation.

3. Using PL/SQL: If you are using PL/SQL, you can use the DBMS_LOB package to read the contents of a BLOB column. Here's an example:

   DECLARE
     l_blob BLOB;
     l_buffer RAW(32767);
     l_amount BINARY_INTEGER := 32767;
     l_pos INTEGER := 1;
   BEGIN
     SELECT blob_column INTO l_blob FROM table_name WHERE <condition>;
     WHILE l_amount >= 32767 LOOP
       DBMS_LOB.READ(l_blob, l_amount, l_pos, l_buffer);
       -- Do something with l_buffer (e.g. write it to a file)
       l_pos := l_pos + l_amount;
     END LOOP;
     IF l_amount > 0 THEN
       DBMS_LOB.READ(l_blob, l_amount, l_pos, l_buffer);
       -- Do something with the remaining data in l_buffer
     END IF;
   END;

   This code reads the contents of the BLOB column into a buffer and then processes it in chunks of 32767 bytes (the maximum size of a RAW variable in PL/SQL). You can adjust the buffer size and the processing logic according to your needs.

