You can use a custom JsonConverter to handle the serialization and deserialization of your LikeType classes. Here is an implementation:

```
public class LikeTypeConverter<T> : JsonConverter
{
    public override void WriteJson(JsonWriter writer, object value, JsonSerializer serializer)
    {
        serializer.Serialize(writer, ((LikeType<T>)value).Value);
    }

    public override object ReadJson(JsonReader reader, Type objectType, object existingValue, JsonSerializer serializer)
    {
        var value = serializer.Deserialize<T>(reader);
        return Activator.CreateInstance(objectType, value);
    }

    public override bool CanConvert(Type objectType)
    {
        return objectType.IsSubclassOf(typeof(LikeType<>));
    }
}
```

You can then apply this converter to your Name class like this:

```
[JsonConverter(typeof(LikeTypeConverter<string>))]
public class Name : LikeType<string>
{
    public Name(string s) : base(s) { }
}
```

Now when you serialize a Name object to JSON, it will be serialized as a string:

```
var name = new Name("john");
var json = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(name);
// json == {"Value":"john"}
```

And when you deserialize that JSON back into a Name object, it will be converted back to a Name object with the Value property set to the deserialized string.

```
var name2 = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<Name>(json);
// name2.Value == "john"
```

